/*
	RMSTEST 18 — is min_distance_group_placement enforced during a tight
	group's FILL, or only once per group?

	preview-design Sec.15 item 10, the last open engine question. Sec.6.6 now
	states a uniform rule from three measurements: under tight grouping the
	ANCHOR is checked against every constraint and the fill is checked against
	nothing. The three tested were avoid_forest_zone (distance avoidance),
	terrain_to_place_on (tile validity) and avoid_actor_area (area exclusion).

	min_distance_group_placement is a different SHAPE of constraint. The other
	three ask "is this tile acceptable"; this one asks "is this tile far enough
	from other placements". It is the one candidate that could plausibly be
	enforced per member while the others are not, which would give the uniform
	rule its first exception.

	guide:2611 defines it as spacing between individual objects of the same
	command and all future ones; guide:2614 then says "If the objects are
	grouped, distance refers to the center of the group, not the individual
	members." So the guide and our measured rule agree here, and this test is a
	confirmation rather than a coin flip — worth running precisely because
	agreement predicted in advance is the cheapest kind of evidence to get.

	SETUP: six tight groups of 49 (7x7 blobs) with a required spacing of 12.

	WHAT TO MEASURE — the gap between BLOBS, not the object count. Tight groups
	are contiguous, so the gold forms six clumps that can be recovered by
	spatial clustering; group membership is not stored in the scenario file and
	does not need to be.

	  minimum edge-to-edge gap between blobs clearly BELOW 12 (roughly 0-6)
	      -> the constraint is applied once per group. The uniform rule holds
	         for a fourth constraint, now spanning four distinct kinds, and
	         guide:2614 is right. Sec.15 item 10 closes.
	  minimum gap at or ABOVE 12 on every pair
	      -> enforced per member. First exception to the uniform rule; Sec.6.6
	         needs a carve-out naming this attribute and the reason, and
	         temp_min_distance_group_placement then needs the same test.
	  FEWER than six blobs
	      -> two groups merged into one contiguous mass, which is an even
	         stronger once-per-group signal: their members are at distance 1.
	         Report the count.

	Send the export and the analysis will be run against it; no manual counting.

	DESIGN NOTES
	  Flat all-grass map with no terrain, elevation or constraints other than
	  the one under test, so nothing else can influence where the groups land.
	  49 per group is a size already known to place reliably (RMSTEST_8 placed
	  25 tight with no trouble) while being wide enough that a 12-tile spacing
	  measured at the anchor leaves blob edges visibly closer than 12.
	  Six groups on Small is 294 objects on 20736 tiles — dense enough that the
	  spacing constraint actually bites, far from dense enough to run out of
	  room.
	  No reference frame, so set_gaia_object_only is not required (Test 3
	  confirmed that requirement is frame-scoped) and no frame logic can
	  interfere.
	  Player lands are tiny and pushed to the rim so they cannot crowd the
	  placement area.

	RUN ON: Small, 2 players. Twice if convenient — the placement is random and
	a second run guards against one lucky arrangement.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain GRASS

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 3
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 45 0
}

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

create_object GOLD
{
	number_of_objects 49
	number_of_groups 6
	set_tight_grouping
	min_distance_group_placement 12
}
